lan_3.md` in full first: it is the plan this loop   executes and §0 lists what the three earlier loops established. Then read   the sections of `reports/ml_nature.md` your item cites, the one-page §0 of   `reports/ml_nature_summary.md`, and `CLAUDE.md`'s blocks \"What the corpus   says about the problem itself\", \"The generated-ensemble campaign\" and \"The   objects loop\". `reports/learning_plan.md` §4 and `reports/ml_nature_summary.md`   §9 list what is closed and must not be rebuilt. - **A prediction is never a bound.** Nothing learned may touch   `satisfiability/mosp_solver.py::_lower_bound` or any path that decides `k`.   A candidate bound is checked on every certified instance, attacked by the   extremal search, written up as a conjecture, and never used by the solver. - **No solver default changes.** Fix variants (item 04), new heuristics   (items 01, 02) and any flag are measured behind options that default to   today's behaviour, with paired node counts, and *proposed* in the report.   A new heuristic strategy may be registered in `satisfiability/heuristics.py`   as a named option; it may not become the default of anything. - **Every split groups by file ∪ MOSP-graph isomorphism class**   (`learning.fingerprint.union_groups`). - **Nodes, not seconds**; seconds beside them; a censored call is a lower   bound, never a missing value; SAT cost is counted in conflicts. - **State the size range every conclusion covers.** Corpus 6,376 at 9–134;   campaign 37,800 at 10–40, 6,747 at 50–75, a 5-per-cell sample at 100; item   07 adds one certified cell at 100. Nothing below 100 is evidence about   125 × 125 except through a stated extrapolation. - Every number in a report is regenerated by a command named in that report. - Findings that rest on a handful of instances are re-certified for those   instances (`solve_mosp_exact`, never `solutions_dir=None`) before being written.  - **Compute**: check `pgrep -f benchmarks.recertify`; if it is running, its   cores are not